# Target C-II — Pareto refinement reduces the adjacent finite cusp set below `1800` rows

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** the audited `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844` full-core Type-II adjacent-cut backbone  
**Dependencies:** `target_c_adjacent_cut_triple_small_2026-09-15.md` and its hostile audit; exact laminar O--O interval support; audited global strict-rhombus angular-defect budget  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_adjacent_cut_pareto_refinement.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The audited triple-small theorem chooses one internal boundary of a linear good-leaf
backbone by averaging the three costs

\[
N_i,
\qquad
D B_i,
\qquad
D(q_i+q_{i+1})
\]

with equal weight.  This gives the balanced bound `21200` for all three quantities.
For the downstream finite-cusp problem, however, the number `N_i` of actual diameter
rows that start or stop at the boundary is more important than the two numerical
constants multiplying quantities already known to be `O(D^{-1})`.

A direct union bound gives a stronger Pareto statement and, in particular, cuts the
bounded exceptional diameter set by more than a factor of eleven.

## Theorem PR — adjacent-cut Pareto refinement

Use the notation and selected path

\[
P=(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m)
\]

from the audited triple-small theorem.  Put

\[
p={278778993\over78216868750}
=0.0035641799\ldots,
\]

so

\[
m>pD-o(D).
\]

For every internal boundary define

\[
N_i
=
\#\{h:\varepsilon_{h,i+1}\ne\varepsilon_{hi}\},
\]

\[
B_i
=
\sum_h\beta_h
|\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}|,
\]

and

\[
Q_i=q_i+q_{i+1}.
\]

The parent proof gives the three independent global budgets

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2\pi D,
\tag{PR.1}
\]

\[
\sum_i D B_i\le2D,
\tag{PR.2}
\]

and

\[
\sum_i DQ_i
\le {2C_{phase}\over d_{low}}D+o(D),
\tag{PR.3}
\]

where

\[
d_{low}={9891\over62500}=0.158256
\]

and

\[
C_{phase}
={\pi^2\sqrt3(2+\sqrt3)\over12}.
\]

More generally, if fixed positive thresholds `n,b,a` satisfy

\[
\boxed{
{2\pi\over n}
+{2\over b}
+{2C_{phase}\over d_{low}a}
<p,
}
\tag{PR.4}
\]

then one internal boundary satisfies simultaneously

\[
N_i<n+o(1),
\qquad
DB_i<b+o(1),
\qquad
DQ_i<a+o(1).
\tag{PR.5}
\]

Taking

\[
\boxed{
n=1800,
\qquad b=a=10^6
}
\tag{PR.6}
\]

is valid.  Hence there are consecutive smallest-leaf O--O cuts
`tau_i,tau_{i+1}` on the same selected path such that

\[
\boxed{N_i<1800+o(1),}
\tag{PR.7}
\]

\[
\boxed{B_i<{10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}),}
\tag{PR.8}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
< {10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).}
\tag{PR.9}
\]

The last implication uses the parent inequality

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
\le q_i+q_{i+1}=Q_i.
\]

Thus the adjacent opposite-shear reduction can be run with fewer than `1800+o(1)`
large first-order diameter rows, rather than `21200+o(1)`.

---

## 1. Union-bound proof

Call an internal boundary count-bad if `N_i>=n`, multiplier-bad if `DB_i>=b`, and
phase-bad if `DQ_i>=a`.

By (PR.1), the number of count-bad boundaries is at most

\[
{2\pi D\over n}.
\tag{PR.10}
\]

By (PR.2), the number of multiplier-bad boundaries is at most

\[
{2D\over b}.
\tag{PR.11}
\]

By (PR.3), the number of phase-bad boundaries is at most

\[
{2C_{phase}\over d_{low}a}D+o(D).
\tag{PR.12}
\]

If every internal boundary were bad in at least one sense, then the path would have at
most the sum of (PR.10)--(PR.12) internal boundaries.  Condition (PR.4) makes that
strictly smaller than `pD-o(D)`, contradicting the path-length theorem.  Therefore one
boundary is good in all three senses.  This proves (PR.5).

The argument does not change the path, the center train producing it, or any KKT
normalization.  It only replaces the equal-weight averaging step by a three-set union
bound.

---

## 2. Exact instantiation at `1800,10^6,10^6`

Use the same outward rational enclosures as the parent certificate,

\[
\pi<{355\over113},
\]

\[
C_{phase}
<{3258689123681\over612912000000}.
\]

The companion exact verifier evaluates the three bad-boundary densities as

\[
{2(355/113)\over1800}
=0.00349065880039\ldots,
\]

\[
{2\over10^6}=0.000002,
\]

and

\[
{2C_{phase}^{up}\over d_{low}10^6}
=0.00006719154354\ldots .
\]

Their sum is

\[
0.00355985034393\ldots,
\]

whereas

\[
p=0.00356417991995\ldots .
\]

The exact rational gap is greater than

\[
\boxed{4\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{PR.13}
\]

So the advertised thresholds have real margin and do not rely on floating-point
rounding.

---

## 3. Consequence for the finite-cusp normal form

Choose the projective sign `s in {+1,-1}` as in the audited finite-cusp theorem and
apply the exact opposite shear

\[
(\theta_i,\theta_{i+1})=(t,-st).
\]

For every actual diameter row spanning both cuts, the first relative-displacement
coefficient is

\[
J(u_i-su_{i+1}).
\]

Equation (PR.9) gives

\[
|u_i-su_{i+1}|
<{10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
\]

so every non-switching spanning diameter row has squared-length first derivative
bounded by

\[
2\times10^6+o(1).
\tag{PR.14}
\]

Only the rows counted by `N_i` can have the natural `O(D)` first-derivative scale.
Therefore the same exact finite multishear now has

\[
\boxed{<1800+o(1)}
\]

large first-order active diameter rows.

Their total normalized KKT multiplier is bounded by (PR.8), and hence their total
KKT-weighted absolute squared-length first derivative is at most

\[
2D B_i
<2\times10^6+o(1).
\tag{PR.15}
\]

At finite amplitude `t=xi/D` with fixed `xi`, the parent Taylor argument is unchanged:
all non-switching rows change by `O(D^{-1})`, while only this `<1800+o(1)` switching set
can change by `O(1)`.

This does not evade the hostile-audited local-jamming obstruction.  The switching rows
remain exact active max constraints even when their total KKT multiplier is small.
The gain is combinatorial: any downstream diameter-thrackle/interface classification
now has fewer than `1800` exceptional rows to understand, instead of fewer than
`21200`.

---

## 4. Frontier effect

The balanced `21200` theorem remains useful when comparable constants are desired.
The present result is a separate Pareto strengthening optimized for finite blocker
classification:

```text
same linear good-leaf backbone
+ same exact adjacent O-O cut pair framework
+ fewer than 1800 switching diameter rows
+ switching beta mass = O(1/D)
+ phase mismatch = O(1/D)
+ exact opposite multishear still available
```

The remaining obstruction is unchanged in kind but substantially smaller in finite
size.  A closing theorem still needs an impossible diameter-thrackle/interface pattern,
a nonlocal same-cardinality replacement, or an enlarged coordinate family that leaves
the pure old-contact O-O shear cusp.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only Pareto strengthening of the adjacent finite-cusp
normal form. C-II and Level I remain open.
